    {
      "id": "library/9789241549912-eng/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-1-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Chapter 1. Introduction\n1. Introduction\n1.1 Background\nInternational human rights law includes fundamental \ncommitments of states to enable women and \nadolescent girls to survive pregnancy and childbirth \nas part of their enjoyment of sexual and reproductive \nhealth and rights and living a life of dignity (1). The \nWorld Health Organization (WHO) envisions a world \nwhere “every pregnant woman and newborn receives \nquality care throughout the pregnancy, childbirth and \nthe postnatal period” (2). However, approximately \n303 000 women and adolescent girls died as a result \nof pregnancy and childb",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Chapter 1 (Introduction) establishing the background, rationale, target audience, and scope of the 2016 WHO ANC guideline. Reviews global maternal mortality (303,000 deaths in 2015) and stillbirth burdens (2.6 million in 2015), explains why the 2002 4-visit FANC model proved insufficient and how vertical disease programmes fragmented routine ANC, situates the guideline within the SDGs and Every Woman Every Child Global Strategy, defines the person-centred and human-rights-based concept of a positive pregnancy experience, specifies the adolescent and adult pregnant population and health-system audience, and lists the priority maternal, fetal/neonatal, test-accuracy, and health-system outcomes in Box 1.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-28"
      }
