      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "A. Nutritional interventions",
      "pageStart": 31,
      "pageEnd": 58,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-a-nutritional-interventions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "B.\tMaternal and fetal assessment\nC.\tPreventive measures\nD.\tInterventions for common physiological \nsymptoms\nE.\tHealth systems interventions to improve the \nutilization and quality of ANC.\nThe corresponding GRADE tables for \nrecommendations are referred to in this chapter as \n“evidence base” (EB) tables, numbered according \nto the specific recommendations they refer to. \nThese tables are presented separately in the \nWeb supplement to this document.3 Evidence-to-\ndecision tables with GDG judgements related to \nthe evidence and considerations for all domains \nare presented in Web annex 3 of this",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Chapter 3, Section A (Nutritional interventions), presenting background epidemiology on maternal malnutrition, overweight/obesity, and anaemia alongside evidence summaries, DECIDE EtD considerations (values, resources, equity, acceptability, feasibility), and implementation remarks for 14 nutritional recommendations: A.1.1 counselling on healthy eating and physical activity (recommended), A.1.2 nutrition education on increasing daily energy and protein intake in undernourished populations (context-specific), A.1.3 balanced energy and protein dietary supplementation in undernourished populations (context-specific), A.1.4 high-protein supplementation (not recommended), A.2.1 daily oral iron (30–60 mg) and folic acid (400 µg) supplementation (recommended), A.2.2 intermittent oral iron (120 mg) and folic acid (2800 µg) weekly where daily iron is unacceptable or anaemia prevalence is <20% (context-specific), A.3 daily calcium supplementation (1.5–2.0 g) in low-calcium-intake populations (context-specific), A.4 vitamin A supplementation where vitamin A deficiency is a severe public health problem (context-specific), A.5 zinc supplementation (context-specific research), A.6 multiple micronutrient supplements (not recommended), A.7 vitamin B6 supplements (not recommended), A.8 vitamin E and C supplements (not recommended), A.9 vitamin D supplements (not recommended), and A.10 lowering daily caffeine intake for women consuming >300 mg/day (context-specific).",
        "state": "draft",
