    {
      "id": "library/9789241549912-eng/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "C. Preventive measures",
      "pageStart": 81,
      "pageEnd": 123,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-c-preventive-measures.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "C. Preventive measures\nBackground\nThe GDG considered the evidence and other relevant \ninformation to inform recommendations on antenatal \ninterventions to prevent the following conditions.\nn\nn Asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB): Defined as true \nbacteriuria in the absence of specific symptoms \nof acute urinary tract infection, ASB is common \nin pregnancy, with rates as high as 74% reported \nin some LMICs (125). Escherichia coli is associated \nwith up to 80% of isolates (83). Other pathogens \ninclude Klebsiella species, Proteus mirabilis and \ngroup B streptococcus (GBS). While ASB in non-\npregnant",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Chapter 3, Sections C (Preventive measures), D (Interventions for common physiological symptoms), and E (Health systems interventions to improve utilization and quality of ANC), plus the opening of Chapter 4. Section C covers C.1 a 7-day antibiotic regimen for asymptomatic bacteriuria (recommended), C.2 antibiotic prophylaxis for recurrent UTIs (context-specific research), C.3 antenatal anti-D immunoglobulin prophylaxis at 28 and 34 weeks in non-sensitized Rh-negative women (context-specific research), C.4 single-dose albendazole or mebendazole after the first trimester where hookworm/T. trichiura prevalence is ≥20% and anaemia is ≥40% (context-specific), C.5 tetanus toxoid vaccination (recommended), C.6 intermittent preventive treatment with sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine (IPTp-SP) starting in the second trimester at least one month apart for ≥3 doses in malaria-endemic areas in Africa (context-specific, integrated), and C.7 oral TDF-based pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) for pregnant women at substantial HIV risk (context-specific, integrated). Section D covers D.1 ginger, chamomile, vitamin B6, and/or acupuncture for nausea in early pregnancy, D.2 diet/lifestyle advice and antacids for heartburn, D.3 magnesium, calcium, or non-pharmacological options for leg cramps, D.4 regular exercise plus physiotherapy, support belts, and acupuncture for low back and pelvic pain, D.5 wheat bran or fibre supplements for constipation, and D.6 compression stockings, leg elevation, and water immersion for varicose veins and oedema (all recommended based on women's preferences). Section E covers E.1 woman-held case notes (recommended), E.2 midwife-led continuity of care (MLCC) in settings with well-functioning midwifery programmes (context-specific), E.3 group ANC in research settings (context-specific research), E.4.1 facilitated participatory learning and action (PLA) cycles with women's groups and E.4.2 household/community mobilization with antenatal home visits in rural low-access settings (both context-specific), E.5.1–E.5.2 task shifting health promotion and nutritional/IPTp supplement distribution across health worker cadres (recommended, with Box 4 comparing OptimizeMNH and ANC recommendations), E.6 educational, regulatory, financial, and professional support interventions to recruit and retain rural staff (context-specific, integrated), and E.7 ANC models with a minimum of eight contacts to reduce perinatal mortality and improve women's experience of care (recommended).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
